{"from": "artifact:herod-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-01-herod-king", "grade": "A", "note": "Coins minted under Herod the Great (37-4 BCE) bear the Greek inscription ΗΡΩΔΟΥ ΒΑΣΙΛΕΩΣ, 'of King Herod'."}
{"from": "artifact:herod-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-the-great", "grade": "A", "note": "Coins inscribed 'of King Herod' name Herod and his royal title."}
{"from": "site:herodium", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-the-great", "grade": "B", "note": "Herod's palace-fortress and mausoleum at Herodium; an ornate sarcophagus found in 2007 was claimed to be Herod's, without an inscription."}
{"from": "site:herodium", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-01-herod-king", "grade": "B", "note": "The Herodium palace-fortress complex shows Herod's royal power and building programme in Judea."}
{"from": "artifact:bethlehem-bulla", "type": "attests", "to": "place:bethlehem", "grade": "C", "note": "A First Temple-period seal impression reading 'From the town of Bethlehem to the King', found 2012; names Bethlehem centuries before the New Testament, not evidence for the first-century town."}
{"from": "artifact:bethlehem-bulla", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-05-micah-bethlehem", "grade": "C", "note": "The bulla confirms Bethlehem as a real Judean town of the Kingdom of Judah, the town the prophecy names."}
{"from": "site:church-of-the-nativity", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-01-birth-in-bethlehem", "grade": "D", "note": "Commemorative tradition: a Bethlehem cave was pointed out as the birthplace by the 3rd century (Origen). It attests the tradition, not the event itself."}
{"from": "site:church-of-the-nativity", "type": "attests", "to": "place:bethlehem", "grade": "B", "note": "The oldest major church in the Holy Land stands at Bethlehem over the venerated grotto."}
{"from": "text:origen-contra-celsum", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-01-birth-in-bethlehem", "grade": "D", "note": "Origen (c. 248) records that a Bethlehem cave was shown as Jesus' birthplace - early Christian tradition, not independent evidence of the birth."}
{"from": "artifact:archelaus-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-22-archelaus-reigns", "grade": "A", "note": "Coins of Herod Archelaus (4 BCE-6 CE) bear his name (ΗΡΩΔ) and title Ethnarch (ΕΘΝ) in Greek."}
{"from": "artifact:archelaus-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-archelaus", "grade": "A", "note": "Coins inscribed with Archelaus's name and the title Ethnarch."}
{"from": "artifact:archelaus-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "place:judea", "grade": "C", "note": "The ethnarch's coins circulated in the districts he ruled, Judea, Samaria and Idumea."}
{"from": "artifact:archelaus-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-19-herod-died", "grade": "C", "note": "Archelaus's coinage is dated from 4 BCE, consistent with Herod's death then; the exact year is disputed (some argue 1 BCE)."}
{"from": "artifact:caesarea-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "place:nazareth", "grade": "A", "note": "The first non-Christian reference to Nazareth: a Hebrew inscription from Caesarea naming Nazareth as the home of the priestly Hapizzez/Hafizaz family."}
{"from": "artifact:caesarea-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-23-settled-nazareth", "grade": "C", "note": "The inscription names Nazareth as a village of priestly settlement, though centuries after the New Testament period (after 135 CE)."}
{"from": "artifact:caesarea-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "place:galilee", "grade": "C", "note": "The inscription lists the twenty-four priestly courses and the town or village in Galilee where each settled."}
{"from": "site:nazareth-house", "type": "attests", "to": "place:nazareth", "grade": "B", "note": "Excavated remains at Nazareth dating to the early Roman period, the time of Jesus."}
{"from": "site:nazareth-house", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-23-settled-nazareth", "grade": "B", "note": "A first-century dwelling excavated at Nazareth confirms an inhabited village there in the early Roman period."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-death", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-19-herod-died", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus narrates Herod's death, preceded by a lunar eclipse and a fast day; the same event Matthew refers to."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-death", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-the-great", "grade": "A", "note": "Book 17 of the Antiquities narrates Herod's last illness, death and funeral arrangements."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-death", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-01-herod-king", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus records Herod dying as reigning king, five days after Antipater's execution, and the division of his kingdom."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-death", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-04-chief-priests-consulted", "grade": "C", "note": "Context: Josephus shows Herod appointing and deposing high priests in his last days, confirming the priestly establishment he could consult."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-death", "type": "attests", "to": "place:jerusalem", "grade": "C", "note": "Context: the account of the high priesthood, the Temple fast day and the sedition Herod crushed presupposes the Jerusalem establishment."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-archelaus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-22-archelaus-reigns", "grade": "A", "note": "Caesar appointed Archelaus ethnarch of half of Herod's kingdom; Josephus narrates his rule and banishment to Vienna (Vienne) in Gaul."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-archelaus", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-archelaus", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus names Archelaus as ethnarch and recounts his banishment."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-archelaus", "type": "attests", "to": "place:judea", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus describes Judea as the core of the half of Herod's kingdom given to Archelaus."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-cruelty", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-16-massacre", "grade": "C", "note": "Context only: Josephus records that dying Herod ordered one man out of every family slain, so that the whole nation should mourn; he never records a massacre at Bethlehem."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-cruelty", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mt-02-16-massacre", "note": "Josephus, who details Herod's atrocities including the murder of three of his own sons, does not mention the massacre of Bethlehem's children."}
{"from": "text:herodotus-1-101", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-01-magi-from-east", "grade": "C", "note": "Herodotus (1.101) lists the Magi as one of the six tribes of the Medes - the priestly class behind the Greek word 'magos'."}
{"from": "text:macrobius-saturnalia", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-16-massacre", "grade": "D", "note": "Macrobius (early 5th century) reports Herod ordered boys in Syria under two killed; discounted as extra-biblical evidence due to later authorship."}
{"from": "text:macrobius-saturnalia", "type": "attests", "to": "person:herod-the-great", "grade": "C", "note": "Macrobius calls Herod 'king of the Jews' in a late, confused anecdote."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-13-onias", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-02-13-flight-to-egypt", "grade": "C", "note": "Context: Egypt hosted a Jewish temple at Leontopolis (built by Onias) and long-standing Jewish communities, so a Jewish family had somewhere to go."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-13-onias", "type": "attests", "to": "place:egypt", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus describes Onias building a temple in Egypt like the one at Jerusalem."}
